---
import BaseHead from '../../components/BaseHead.astro';
import ProjectHeader from '../../components/ProjectHeader.astro';
import ProjectFooter from '../../components/ProjectFooter.astro';
import PBRCapture from '../../components/PBRCapture.astro';
import PBRComparison from '../../components/PBRComparison.astro';
const stages = [
  ['01', 'Material inputs', 'Albedo, tangent-space normals, metallic R, smoothness A, occlusion G, and emission.'],
  ['02', 'Direct lighting', 'GGX distribution, Smith joint visibility approximation, Schlick Fresnel, and Disney/Burley diffuse.'],
  ['03', 'Environment lighting', 'Unity SH or lightmaps for diffuse; prefiltered reflection probes for specular.'],
  ['04', 'Composition', 'Light attenuation and shadows, indirect diffuse and specular, emission, then fog.'],
];
const fixes = [
  ['Visibility convention', 'Replaced the incorrect geometry-term expression with a Smith joint GGX visibility approximation that incorporates geometric masking and shadowing together with denominator normalization, giving a specular BRDF of D × V × F.'],
  ['Roughness stability', 'Separated perceptual roughness from GGX α and added a lower bound for numerical stability.'],
  ['Probe blending', 'Corrected the probe-weight check and guarded the blending path with Unity’s feature macros.'],
  ['Environment response', 'Added the surface-reduction factor to the environment specular response.'],
];
---
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head><BaseHead title="Unity PBR Shader Study — Chao Bian" description="A Unity Built-in rendering study: implementing direct-light BRDFs, integrating environment lighting, and debugging material inputs against Unity Standard." /></head>
  <body>
    <a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to content</a>
    <ProjectHeader />
    <main id="main">
      <header class="hero wrap">
        <div class="kicker"><span>COMPUTER GRAPHICS / RENDERING STUDY</span><span>UNITY · HLSL / CG</span></div>
        <h1>Unity PBR<br />Shader Study<span>From BRDF to material response.</span></h1>
        <div class="hero-copy"><p>Implementing a physically based shader in Unity, integrating environment lighting, and tracing visual differences back to the math and material inputs.</p><div class="hero-actions"><a class="button" href="#comparison">Explore the comparison ↘</a><a class="source-link" href="/projects/pbr-shader-source">View source ↗</a></div></div>
        <dl class="metadata"><div><dt>MY WORK</dt><dd>Shader implementation &amp; debugging</dd></div><div><dt>ENVIRONMENT</dt><dd>Unity 2022.3 · Built-in · Linear</dd></div><div><dt>REFERENCE</dt><dd>Tutorials &amp; Unity Standard source</dd></div></dl>
        <figure class="hero-render"><a href="/projects/pbr-shader/custom-bronze.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="/projects/pbr-shader/custom-bronze.png" alt="Refined bronze Corinthian helmet reflecting the Mirrored Hall environment" width="1920" height="1080" fetchpriority="high" /></a><figcaption><span>Bronze / Custom PBR · Mirrored Hall environment</span><a href="/projects/pbr-shader/custom-bronze.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Full capture ↗</a></figcaption></figure>
      </header>

      <nav class="chapter-nav" aria-label="Project sections"><div class="wrap"><a href="#comparison">00 / Comparison</a><a href="#overview">01 / Overview</a><a href="#lighting">02 / Lighting</a><a href="#debugging">03 / Debugging</a><a href="#reflection">04 / Takeaways</a></div></nav>

      <section id="comparison" class="wrap chapter">
        <p class="eyebrow">00 / Visual reference</p><h2>Comparing with<br />Unity Standard.</h2>
        <p class="intro">After aligning the shading conventions and smoothness inputs, the helmet materials produced a close visual match in the tested scene.</p>
        <div class="comparison-grid"><PBRComparison material="Bronze" custom="/projects/pbr-shader/custom-bronze.png" standard="/projects/pbr-shader/standard-bronze.png" id="bronze-comparison" /><PBRComparison material="Oxidized metal" custom="/projects/pbr-shader/custom-rust.png" standard="/projects/pbr-shader/standard-rust.png" id="rust-comparison" /></div>
        <p class="comparison-note">The tested materials and scene show a close visual match, with subtle local brightness differences remaining along the lower cheek guards, most visibly in bronze. Both pairs use the same crop and scale; the implementation focuses on opaque ForwardBase shading.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="overview" class="wrap chapter overview">
        <div><p class="eyebrow">01 / The project</p><h2>Understanding what<br />makes a material<br />look right.</h2></div>
        <div><p class="intro">This study connects the microfacet BRDF to a working real-time material, using Unity Standard as a visual and implementation reference.</p><p>I developed two shaders from tutorial material and refined them against Unity’s Built-in implementation. <code>PBR_simple</code> evaluates direct lighting with a simple ambient term. <code>PBR_IBL</code> extends the same material model with Unity’s environment-lighting data.</p><p>My work covers the shading logic, material input conventions, and debugging. Unity supplies the lightmaps, SH coefficients, and prefiltered probe textures; I implemented how the shader reads and combines them.</p></div>
      </section>

      <section id="lighting" class="dark">
        <div class="wrap chapter">
          <p class="eyebrow">02 / Implementation</p><h2>One material.<br />Two sources of light.</h2>
          <p class="intro">Direct light is evaluated from the scene light. Environment light is reconstructed from data prepared by Unity, with the material still controlling the response at runtime.</p>
          <ol class="pipeline">{stages.map(([number, heading, description]) => <li><span>{number}</span><h3>{heading}</h3><p>{description}</p></li>)}</ol>
          <div class="columns lighting-details"><article><p class="eyebrow">DIRECT / BRDF CONVENTION</p><h3>The microfacet model</h3><div class="formula">f<sub>specular</sub> = D × V × F</div><p>V already includes G / (4 N·L N·V), so this denominator is applied once.</p></article><article><p class="eyebrow">ENVIRONMENT / DATA FLOW</p><h3>From stored light to shading</h3><div class="light-flow" aria-label="SH or lightmap supplies indirect diffuse; reflection probe supplies indirect specular"><div><span>SH / Lightmap</span><span aria-hidden="true">→</span><strong>Indirect diffuse</strong></div><div><span>Reflection Probe</span><span aria-hidden="true">→</span><strong>Indirect specular</strong></div></div></article></div>
          <div class="equation">Final color = direct lighting + indirect diffuse + indirect specular + emission</div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="debugging" class="tinted"><div class="wrap chapter">
        <p class="eyebrow">03 / A debugging case</p><h2>The BRDF was only<br />part of the difference.</h2>
        <p class="intro">A material input mismatch explained an important part of the visual gap.</p>
        <div class="columns debugging"><div><h3>Trace the material input.</h3><ol class="steps"><li><strong>Problem</strong><p>The metal helmet had sharper reflections and deeper dark regions than Standard.</p></li><li><strong>Cause</strong><p>The custom shader read roughness from a separate texture’s R channel; Standard used Metallic Alpha × Smoothness. The resulting roughness values differed.</p></li><li><strong>Fix &amp; result</strong><p>Aligning Metallic Alpha × Smoothness narrowed the visual gap, alongside the BRDF and environment-response corrections.</p></li></ol></div><div class="code-study"><p class="eyebrow">THE MATERIAL CONVENTION</p><h3>Aligning the<br />smoothness input.</h3><pre><code>{`smoothness = metallicSample.a * _Smoothness;
roughness = 1.0 - smoothness;
alpha = max(roughness * roughness, 0.002);`}</code></pre><p>With a varying Alpha channel, Smoothness = 0.75 does not mean a constant roughness of 0.25.</p></div></div>
        <div class="columns media-pair"><PBRCapture src="/projects/pbr-shader/initial.png" title="Initial implementation" alt="Initial bronze helmet with sharp, concentrated highlights and deeper dark regions" /><PBRCapture src="/projects/pbr-shader/custom-bronze.png" title="Refined implementation" alt="Refined bronze helmet with broader reflections and more continuous surface shading" /></div>
        <p class="capture-caption">Overall iteration: visibility, environment-response, and smoothness-input adjustments. These captures show the combined refinements rather than an isolated smoothness experiment.</p>
        <h3 class="fix-heading">Other corrections in the implementation</h3><div class="fixes">{fixes.map(([heading, description]) => <article><h4>{heading}</h4><p>{description}</p></article>)}</div>
      </div></section>

      <section id="reflection" class="wrap chapter reflection"><p class="eyebrow">04 / What I learned</p><h2>Compare conventions<br />before comparing pixels.</h2><p class="closing">Matching a reference requires consistency across the complete input and calculation flow: texture channels, roughness conventions, the BRDF, and environment reflection.</p><h3 class="connection-title">Connecting to my CPU Path Tracer</h3><p class="project-connection">My CPU Path Tracer samples ray paths and uses Monte Carlo estimation to evaluate light transport. This Unity shader evaluates the main light directly and combines it with environment-lighting data prepared by Unity. Together, the projects helped me understand how similar material models can be evaluated under different computational budgets.</p><a class="related" href="/projects/path-tracer">Related study / CPU Path Tracer ↗</a></section>
      <footer class="wrap credits"><p class="eyebrow">References &amp; scope</p><p>Based on tutorials by Timro: <a href="https://zhuanlan.zhihu.com/p/1893699752635369165">PBR | Unity实现</a> and <a href="https://zhuanlan.zhihu.com/p/1894174112563324389">PBR | IBL 基于图像的光照</a>. Further refinements followed Unity 2022.3 Built-in shader source: <code>UnityStandardBRDF.cginc</code> and <code>UnityGlobalIllumination.cginc</code>.</p><p>Models &amp; textures: <a href="https://assetstore.unity.com/packages/3d/props/clothing/armor/pbr-corinthian-helmets-230928">PBR Corinthian Helmets</a> by Ferocious Industries, via Unity Asset Store.</p><p>HDR environment: <a href="https://polyhaven.com/a/mirrored_hall">Mirrored Hall</a> by Grzegorz Wronkowski, via Poly Haven (CC0).</p><p>My contributions: shader implementation, material-input alignment, and rendering comparisons.</p></footer>
    </main>
    <ProjectFooter />
  </body>
</html>
